# Observability-MCP resource-server vhost (RFC-112 / ADR-114, #56). Deploy seds the real player
# domain in and drops this into /etc/caddy/sites/obs.caddy, then restarts caddy. The SECOND MCP
# on the edge, beside mcp.caddy — same shape, different upstream (127.0.0.1:8848) and a stricter
# gate.
#
# WHY: an external AI agent (claude.ai, Claude Code) reaches the platform's observability control
# plane here to see how prod is doing (metrics/logs/traces/errors/alerts/deploys). The obs MCP
# container enforces bearer auth IN-PROCESS against the app's tailnet-only /internal/mcp/verify —
# and, unlike the content MCP, requires the ADMIN role (APP_MCP_REQUIRE_ADMIN=true), 401s
# otherwise. So this vhost has no coming-soon gate: the admin auth IS the gate.
#
# It serves the PUBLIC RFC 9728 discovery doc /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource un-authed so a
# cold client can walk resource -> authorization server. The authorization server (DCR / authorize
# / token) is the SAME player apex as the content MCP — obs is only the resource.
#
# Rollback: `rm /etc/caddy/sites/obs.caddy && sudo systemctl restart caddy`.
obs.player.example.com {
	import hardened
	# Per-site ACME email — info@<player domain> (the deploy seds player.example.com).
	tls info@player.example.com

	# ONLY the MCP endpoint + the public RFC 9728 discovery doc are exposed here (default-deny 404
	# for anything else). Belt-and-suspenders: unlike the content mcp, ObsAuthMiddleware wraps the
	# WHOLE obs ASGI app, so every path except the GET discovery doc already 401s in-process — the
	# edge allowlist is a second, independent layer, not the only gate.
	@ops path /mcp /mcp/* /.well-known/*
	handle @ops {
		# Streamable HTTP responses can stream (chunked / SSE), so disable response buffering
		# (flush_interval -1) — otherwise a long tool call would buffer.
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8848 {
			flush_interval -1
		}
	}
	# Default-deny everything else on this public surface.
	handle {
		respond "Not Found" 404
	}
}
